<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seventh Crusade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1248–1254</span> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusades">Crusades</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> was the crusade led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> against Ayyubid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1248–1254. After taking Damietta in 1249, the crusaders advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> but were checked at al-Mansura, and in 1250 Louis and much of his army were captured during the retreat. Louis was ransomed and spent four years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> who had fought the crusaders seized power in Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade.jpg" alt="Seventh Crusade" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume de Saint-Pathus, Vie et miracles de Saint Louis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seventh_crusade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
